The `where_in` filter escapes only the single-quote character (via `value.replace(mark, mark * 2)`) and then wraps the value in single quotes. This is not dialect-aware: for backslash-escaped dialects (MySQL/MariaDB/PostgreSQL), a backslash before a quote defeats the doubling, and non-string values are emitted via `str(value)` with no quoting at all. The values come from user-controlled filter values (`filter_values`/`get_filters`) and are rendered into SQL text that is executed, not bound.

